                        "glosses": [
                            "An instance or state of being disjoint; separation."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "b82c8311-70d3-43aa-9ee2-34e24901b1a0",
                                "sense_id": "c57633ce-d082-3a24-a558-eb1be43e9147",
                                "text": "With Henry's divorce and the creation of an autonomous Church of England, Italian intercourse with England entered into a period of disjointure that would continue, except for a brief revival under Mary, .through the end of Elizabeth's reign."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "caad8c63-d358-44e1-8136-125a3347d13a",
                                "sense_id": "c57633ce-d082-3a24-a558-eb1be43e9147",
                                "text": "To distinguish between the two opposing possibilities of this disjointure, or disjunction – between 'injustice' and the opening to the other – a deconstructive move becomes necessary."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "9a8ec635-8dca-4c67-b362-18ebf2688fb7",
                                "sense_id": "c57633ce-d082-3a24-a558-eb1be43e9147",
                                "text": "Theodor Adorno argued that what a given poem seems to say in a literal sense is often contradicted by its formal disjointures, by what he called a poem's parataxis, and that its truth content must be seen as illusory in a special sense."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
